    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i was reading about sourdough starter and got a bit confused about the actual biology. can u explain how the lactobacillus and the yeast (saccharomyces cerevisiae) actually work together? like how do they affect the dough differently in terms of the rise and the sour taste?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both organisms rely on anaerobic fermentation to feed on starches, but *Saccharomyces cerevisiae* primarily consumes sugars that result from starch breakdown, producing carbon dioxide which causes the dough to rise. In contrast, *Lactobacillus* ferments both free sugars and lactic acid breakdown products into lactic and acetic acid, creating the tangy sour flavor and robust texture. They work synergistically because the *Lactobacillus* breaks down complex starches into simpler sugars that the yeast can then efficiently consume for expansion.",
