      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, you can think of the bacteria as prepping the food, but their dominance shifts significantly with temperature. In cooler temperatures, typically below 25°C, Lactobacillus grows much faster than yeast, creating a sourer, denser starter before the yeast becomes active. Conversely, warmer environments (26–30°C) favor *Saccharomyces cerevisiae*, leading to a rise dominated by bubbles and a milder, fresh taste due to its rapid metabolic rate. Additionally, the protein percentage and hydration of the flour you choose can either inhibit or support specific strains, requiring you to adjust feeding intervals based on these environmental variables.",
